# ffmpeg — overlay (watermark · PiP · burned text via drawtext)

0.1.0 Use when you need to composite something ON TOP of a video — a logo/watermark, a picture-in-picture inset, or burned-in text via drawtext (positioning, box, fonts, escaping, timed enable). Covers the overlay filter, scaling the overlay, and the drawtext escaping minefield.

You need to put a SECOND visual element on top of the base video:

- *watermark / logo* — a PNG (usually with alpha) in a corner
- *picture-in-picture (PiP)* — a second video inset (webcam,
reaction, B-roll thumbnail)
- *burned text* — a label, lower-third, timestamp, or CTA drawn
directly into the pixels with `drawtext`
- *timed overlays* — element appears only during a time window
(`enable`'between(t,5,10)'=)

NOT for: full-frame subtitles from an SRT/ASS file ([subtitle-burn](subtitle-burn.md) — that's a dedicated caption renderer); resizing the base video itself ([video-scale-crop](video-scale-crop.md)).

`overlay` and `drawtext` require a re-encode of the base video — you cannot stream-copy and composite at the same time.

app :ffmpeg__overlay_watermark_pip_burned_text_via_drawtext do
  title       "ffmpeg — overlay (watermark · PiP · burned text via drawtext)"
  description "0.1.0 Use when you need to composite something ON TOP of a video — a logo/watermark, a picture-in-picture inset, or burned-in text via drawtext (positioning, box, fonts, escaping, timed enable)."
end

## Mental model

- `overlay` composites input #2 onto input #1 at pixel (x,y).
Needs TWO inputs → use `-filter_complex` (not `-vf`) and label the streams.
- `drawtext` renders text into ONE stream → works with `-vf` (no
second input). It is the most escaping-sensitive filter in ffmpeg; read the pitfalls before composing a complex string.
- Position vars available in `overlay`: `W,H` = base (main) size;
`w,h` = overlay size. In `drawtext`: `w,h` = frame size; `text_w,text_h` (alias `tw,th`) = rendered text box size.

## Workflow

### Watermark — logo PNG in a corner

=overlay=x:y= with `main_w/overlay_w` expressions keeps a fixed margin regardless of resolution.

### verify both inputs
```bash
  test -f input.mp4 || { echo "video missing"; exit 1; }
  test -f logo.png  || { echo "logo missing"; exit 1; }
  ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries stream=width,height -of csv=p=0 logo.png
```

### logo bottom-right with a 20px margin
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -i logo.png -filter_complex \
    "[0:v][1:v]overlay=W-w-20:H-h-20" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy -movflags +faststart out.mp4
  # W,H = base video; w,h = logo. Corner cheatsheet:
  #   top-left      10:10
  #   top-right     W-w-10:10
  #   bottom-left   10:H-h-10
  #   bottom-right  W-w-10:H-h-10
  #   centered      (W-w)/2:(H-h)/2
```

### scale the logo to 12% of frame width first, then overlay
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -i logo.png -filter_complex \
    "[1:v]scale=iw*0.5:-1[wm];[0:v][wm]overlay=W-w-20:H-h-20" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy -movflags +faststart out.mp4
  # scale the OVERLAY (label [1:v]) before compositing. -1 keeps aspect
  # (logos are stills, so the odd-dimension rule is relaxed; -2 is safe too).
```

### semi-transparent watermark (50% via colorchannelmixer alpha)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -i logo.png -filter_complex \
    "[1:v]format=rgba,colorchannelmixer=aa=0.5[wm];[0:v][wm]overlay=W-w-20:H-h-20" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy out.mp4
  # format=rgba ensures an alpha plane exists before aa= multiplies it.
```

### Picture-in-picture (PiP) — second video inset

### inset the second video at 1/4 size, top-right
```bash
  ffmpeg -i main.mp4 -i webcam.mp4 -filter_complex \
    "[1:v]scale=iw/4:-2[pip];[0:v][pip]overlay=W-w-20:20" \
    -map 0:a? \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy -movflags +faststart out.mp4
  # audio comes from the MAIN input (0:a?). Overlay only composites video;
  # to MIX both audio tracks see video-audio-mux. -2 keeps PiP dims even.
```

### rounded / circular PiP via an alpha mask (geq)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i main.mp4 -i face.mp4 -filter_complex \
    "[1:v]scale=300:300,format=rgba, \
       geq=lum='p(X,Y)':a='if(lte(hypot(X-150,Y-150),150),255,0)'[circ]; \
     [0:v][circ]overlay=W-w-30:H-h-30" \
    -map 0:a? -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy out.mp4
  # geq builds a circular alpha (radius 150 around center 150,150).
```

### Burned text with drawtext

FIRST check the build has `drawtext` — it requires ffmpeg compiled `--enable-libfreetype`, which some minimal builds (incl. certain Homebrew bottles) omit. `ffmpeg -filters | grep drawtext` → empty means "No such filter: 'drawtext'" at runtime. Fix: install a full build (`brew install ffmpeg` pulls freetype; on Linux the distro package usually has it). If you can't get drawtext, render the text with [[subtitle-burn]] (libass) or composite a pre-rendered text PNG with `overlay` (works in any build).

Two ways to supply the font:
- `fontfile`/abs/path/to/Font.ttf= — explicit, most portable.
- `font`'Arial'= — relies on fontconfig finding it (may differ
by machine). Prefer `fontfile` in scripts.

### find an installed font path (macOS / Linux)
```bash
  fc-list 2>/dev/null | grep -iE "arial|helvetica|dejavu" | head -3
  # macOS without fontconfig: ls /System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial.ttf
  # Linux fallback that's almost always present:
  #   /usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf
```

### bottom-center label with a semi-opaque box
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf \
    "drawtext=fontfile=/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial.ttf: \
      text='Made with Workbooks': \
      fontcolor=white: fontsize=36: \
      box=1: boxcolor=black@0.5: boxborderw=12: \
      x=(w-text_w)/2: y=h-text_h-40" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    -c:a copy out.mp4
  # x=(w-text_w)/2 → horizontally centered. y=h-text_h-40 → 40px above bottom.
  # box=1 + boxcolor=black@0.5 → translucent backing for legibility.
```

### outline instead of a box (borderw + bordercolor)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf \
    "drawtext=fontfile=/path/Font.ttf:text='LIVE': \
      fontcolor=white:fontsize=48: \
      borderw=4:bordercolor=black: \
      x=40:y=40" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a copy out.mp4
```

### long / dynamic text from a file (avoids shell-escaping hell)
```bash
  printf 'Quarterly results: revenue up 24%%' > caption.txt
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf \
    "drawtext=fontfile=/path/Font.ttf:textfile=caption.txt: \
      fontcolor=white:fontsize=32:x=40:y=h-th-40:box=1:boxcolor=black@0.5:boxborderw=10" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a copy out.mp4
  # textfile= reads the string from disk — no need to escape : ' % in the CLI.
```

### live timecode burn-in (drawtext timecode/pts)
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf \
    "drawtext=fontfile=/path/Font.ttf: \
      text='%{pts\:hms}': fontcolor=white:fontsize=28: \
      box=1:boxcolor=black@0.6:boxborderw=8:x=20:y=20" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a copy out.mp4
  # %{pts\:hms} prints H:MM:SS.mmm. The colon inside the % expansion MUST be
  # backslash-escaped, else drawtext reads it as an option separator.
```

### Timed overlays — show only during a window

`enable` takes an expression; the element renders only when it's true. `t` is the timestamp in seconds.

### logo only from t=5s to t=10s
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -i logo.png -filter_complex \
    "[0:v][1:v]overlay=W-w-20:H-h-20:enable='between(t,5,10)'" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a copy out.mp4
```

### lower-third text for the first 8 seconds only
```bash
  ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vf \
    "drawtext=fontfile=/path/Font.ttf:text='Jane Doe — Founder': \
      fontcolor=white:fontsize=34:box=1:boxcolor=black@0.5:boxborderw=12: \
      x=60:y=h-th-80:enable='lt(t,8)'" \
    -c:v libx264 -crf 23 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a copy out.mp4
  # enable expressions: between(t,A,B) | lt(t,N) | gt(t,N) | gte(t,A)*lte(t,B)
```

### confirm the overlay landed (extract a frame inside the window)
```bash
  test -f "$1" || { echo "output missing"; exit 1; }
  ffmpeg -y -ss 6 -i "$1" -frames:v 1 overlay-check.png 2>/dev/null
  echo "✓ overlay-check.png — visually confirm the element is present at t=6s"
```

## Common pitfalls

1. *`-vf` can't take two inputs.* overlay/PiP need two streams →
     use `-filter_complex` with labels (`[0:v][1:v]`). `drawtext` is single-input so `-vf` is fine for it.

2. *drawtext escaping is brutal.* Inside the filter string, `:`
     separates options, `'` delimits values, `%` introduces an expansion, and `\` escapes. A literal colon in text → `'\:'`; a literal percent → `\%` (or `%%` in a shell printf). When in doubt, use =textfile=file.txt= and avoid the CLI escaping entirely.

3. *Font not found = silent fallback or hard fail.* `font`'Arial'=
     depends on fontconfig; on a fresh Linux box Arial may be absent. Prefer `fontfile`/abs/path.ttf= and verify it exists (the `fc-list` pre step). DejaVuSans is the safest Linux default.

3b. *"No such filter: 'drawtext'".* The build lacks `--enable-libfreetype`. Check up front with `ffmpeg -filters | grep drawtext`. Install a full ffmpeg, or fall back to libass ([[subtitle-burn]]) / an overlaid text PNG.

4. *Logo has no alpha → opaque box.* A JPG logo or a flattened PNG
     paints a solid rectangle. Use a PNG WITH transparency; if it lost alpha, =format=rgba= won't recreate it — re-export the asset with a real alpha channel.

5. *Overlay coordinate vars are case-sensitive.* In `overlay`,
     uppercase `W,H` = main video, lowercase `w,h` = overlay. Swap them and the logo flies off-canvas. (In `drawtext`, `w,h` = the frame and `tw,th` = the text box — different namespace.)

6. *PiP/overlay forces a re-encode of the whole base video.* You
     can't `-c copy` while compositing. Budget the encode time and pick a preset (see [[lossy-tradeoffs]]).

7. *Default overlay only runs to the SHORTER input.* If the PiP
     clip is shorter than the base, it vanishes when it ends. Add `overlay`...:eof_action=pass= to keep the base playing, or loop the inset.

## Verification checklist

- [ ] Output exists + plays end-to-end
- [ ] Overlay/text is in the intended CORNER (extract a frame)
- [ ] Watermark alpha respected (no opaque rectangle)
- [ ] Timed overlay present inside the window, absent outside it
- [ ] Text legible over busy footage (box or outline used)
- [ ] Audio preserved (`ffprobe` still shows the audio stream)

